{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And this is the law of the meal-offering.</b> This section, according to its plain sense, adds four commandments concerning the meal-offering: that it must be eaten as unleavened cakes (Leviticus 6:9), that it must be eaten in the court of the Tent of Meeting (ibid.), that every male among the sons of Aaron shall eat it (Leviticus 6:11), and that whatever touches it shall become holy (ibid.). And according to its midrashic interpretation, many novel matters are taught in it: that even the meal-offering of priests, which is not handful-scooped (אינה נקמצת), requires oil and frankincense; and that he must present it before YHWH — that is, the southwestern corner — and toward the front of the altar — that is, the southeastern corner — so it turns out that it requires presentation at the southwestern corner (Zevachim 63); \"and he shall lift from it\" — from what is joined together, that he may not bring one tenth of an ephah in two vessels (Menachot 24); and that he may not make a fixed measure for the handful (Menachot 19); and He said, \"it shall not be baked leavened,\" to draw a comparison of all labors to baking, so that even its kneading and arranging and every single act performed with it constitutes a transgression of a negative commandment for which one receives lashes independently (Menachot 55)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:50:51.033321390Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}